<article class="env-lemma" id="00CU">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00CU">Lemma <span data-tag="00CU">10.9.15</span>.</a> Any submodule $N'$ of $S^{-1}M$ is of the form $S^{-1}N$ for some $N\subset M$. Indeed one can take $N$ to be the inverse image of $N'$ in $M$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $N$ be the inverse image of $N'$ in $M$. Then one can see that $S^{-1}N\supset N'$. To show they are equal, take $x/s$ in $S^{-1}N$, where $s\in S$ and $x\in N$. This yields that $x/1\in N'$. Since $N'$ is an $S^{-1}R$-submodule we have $x/s = x/1\cdot 1/s\in N'$. This finishes the proof. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>